@prefix gsnaugprov: <https://w3id.org/OntoGSN/augmentation/provenance#> .
@prefix gsnaug:     <https://w3id.org/OntoGSN/augmentation#> .
@prefix gsnaugsh:   <https://w3id.org/OntoGSN/augmentation/shapes#> .
@prefix gsnprov:    <https://w3id.org/OntoGSN/provenance#> .
@prefix gsn:        <https://w3id.org/OntoGSN/ontology#> .
@prefix prov:       <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix dc:         <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/> .
@prefix owl:        <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:        <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:       <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:       <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix vann:       <http://purl.org/vocab/vann/> .
@prefix xsd:        <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@base <https://w3id.org/OntoGSN/augmentation/provenance> .

<https://w3id.org/OntoGSN/augmentation/provenance> rdf:type owl:Ontology ;
    owl:imports <https://w3id.org/OntoGSN/provenance> ;
    dc:title "OntoGSN Augmentation - the record"@en ;
    dc:creator "Tomas Bueno Momčilović"@en ;
    dc:publisher "https://www.fortiss.org/"@en ;
    dc:description """Why each term, rule and shape of https://w3id.org/OntoGSN/augmentation exists, and why several
obvious-looking alternatives were rejected.

The gsnprov: vocabulary supplies the backbone, so the SPARQL in provenance/README.md works over
this record unchanged. Two terms are added below for the same reason the alignment record adds
four: gsnprov:SourcePassage is scoped by definition to the GSN Community Standard v3, and most of
what this vocabulary rests on is not the standard. It is an industrial compliance process, the
PROV-O and SHACL specifications, and OntoGSN's own dialectic rules.

Not read by tools/prov_check.py, which validates an explicit list of three files in provenance/.
Statement records here therefore carry no gsnprov:structuralKey: nothing recomputes it, and a key
nothing checks is a key that silently rots. What is checked is augmentation/check.py, which runs
the rules against a fixture and makes every shape fire once.

This record is also where the augmentation's editorial comments live. The Turtle files carry no
prose rationale of their own - only structural section headers, and the annotations a consumer of
the vocabulary reads: labels, definitions, scope notes, usage notes. Anything that explains why an
axiom is written the way it is, or why an obvious alternative was rejected, belongs to a
gsnprov:Rationale here, where it is queryable and where nothing has to be kept in step by hand
across the serialisations."""@en ;
    vann:preferredNamespacePrefix "gsnaugprov" ;
    owl:versionInfo "0.2.0" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://w3id.org/OntoGSN/augmentation> , <https://w3id.org/OntoGSN/provenance> .


#################################################################
#    Local vocabulary
#    Two terms. Everything else is reused from gsnprov:.
#################################################################

gsnaugprov:CitedArtefact rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf prov:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "cited artefact"@en ;
    skos:definition "Something a decision here rests on that is not the GSN Community Standard: a specification, an industrial process model, or a rule already in OntoGSN."@en ;
    skos:note "Mirrors gsnalignprov:CitedArtefact rather than importing it. The alignment record is about ARGO and this one is about a compliance process; a shared class would have to be defined somewhere neither of them owns."@en .

gsnaugprov:locator rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain gsnprov:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "locator"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where in the cited artefact the passage is: a section number, a rule identifier, a diagram step."@en .


